{{!-- Layout --}}
{{!< default }}

{{!--
    Guide Interactive — appears in the Ghost editor's Template dropdown as
    "Guide Interactive".

    Selecting the template IS the switch: nothing here is tag-gated. This
    deliberately omits the sidebar, the sticky share rail, the comments
    block and the .js-infinite-container that post.hbs wraps around the
    article, because a fixed-nav column reader cannot coexist with a
    feed that appends the next post on scroll.

    The full article still renders server-side inside #post-body, so
    crawlers get the plain prose; the component re-lays-it-out after
    paint. Do not fetch a second copy of the content.

    default.hbs:174 loads post.js for any post or page context, so the
    bundle arrives here unchanged.
--}}

{{#contentFor "M4_class_body"}}is-article is-guide-interactive{{/contentFor}}

{{#post}}
<article class="js-post-article {{post_class}}" data-url="{{url}}" data-title="{{title}}" data-id="{{id}}">
    <section class="post container mx-auto my-10 px-4 md:px-0">

        {{!-- Hero: title and subtitle over the ember field.

              article-media is deliberately NOT rendered. The feature
              image stays set on the post, so OpenGraph and Twitter cards
              still carry it -- ghost_head emits og:image from the post,
              not from whether the theme draws it. Here it appears only as
              a heavily dimmed backdrop behind the embers, for texture
              rather than as a picture to look at. Drop the style
              attribute to remove it entirely. --}}
        <div class="ix-hero"{{#if feature_image}} style="--ix-hero-img: url({{img_url feature_image size="xl"}})"{{/if}}>
            <canvas class="ix-hero-fx" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
            {{> "article/article-header"}}
        </div>

        <div id="post-body"
             class="post-body min-w-0 js-post-content{{#match @custom.post_font "=" "font-serif"}} font-serif{{else}} font-sans{{/match}}{{#match @custom.post_font_size "=" "20px"}} text-xl{{else}} text-lg{{/match}}">
            {{content}}
        </div>

    </section>

    {{!-- The outro is a panel, not a tail.

          PBS can run scroll-snap-type:mandatory because its shell hides
          the template's header and footer, so everything that scrolls IS
          a panel and snapping can never strand you. Our page still has
          tags, comments and related reading, and as a short block after
          the stack they sat past the document's maximum scroll offset --
          unreachable under mandatory. Giving them one full-height panel
          of their own is the same answer PBS reaches by deletion. --}}
    <div class="ix-outro">
        {{#if tags}}
        <div class="post-tags buttons justify-center max-w-3xl mx-auto">
            {{#foreach tags}}<a href="{{url}}" title="{{name}}" class="button is-black is-outlined is-small uppercase">{{name}}</a>{{/foreach}}
        </div>
        {{/if}}

        <div class="post-footer container mx-auto max-w-3xl">
            {{#if comments}}
            <div class="gh-comments gh-canvas">
                {{comments}}
            </div>
            {{/if}}
        </div>

        {{> "article/article-related"}}
    </div>
</article>

{{/post}}

{{!-- All behaviour for this template lives in one bundle: the column
      component, the tilt signal and the three.js ember field. Loading it
      here rather than in default.hbs keeps three.js off every other page
      on the site. --}}
{{#contentFor "scripts"}}
<script src="{{asset "scripts/guide.js"}}" defer></script>
{{/contentFor}}
